== WHAT IT IS
    tree struct FileTree:
        path: str
        children: List[FileTree]          the one recursive field

    tree struct Node[T]:                  a parameterized tree
        value: T
        children: List[Node[T]]

A tree struct is an ordinary immutable struct whose one recursive field
holds its ordered children: values of its own type. A value means the
finite rooted ordered tree it describes, built from children that already
exist -- there is no reference, node identity, parent link, cycle or
shared node, and a subtree (`t.children`'s elements, what a walk hands
over) is a value, never a view into its parent. How the runtimes store the
recursion is representation: nothing a program does observes it.

    coroutine {step, first} = std::tree::walk(root, std::tree::Order::BreadthFirst)
    visit(first)
    loop:
        if std::coroutine::done?(step):
            break
        visit(step())

== RULES
  * Exactly one field of type `List[Self]` -- Self the declaration applied
    to its own slots in order (`List[Node[T]]` in `Node[T]`) -- and no
    other mention of the declaration in any field: not `child: Node`,
    `List[List[Node]]`, `MutableVector[Node]`, a map or set of Node, a
    function type over Node, another application (`List[Node[int]]`), or
    mutual recursion through another declaration (TREE-STRUCT-SHAPE). A
    general recursive type is an ordinary `struct` (concept.structs).
  * A tree struct is never opaque, a context or an identity struct
    (TREE-STRUCT-COMBINATION), and never affine: a tree of coroutines, or
    a `Node[T]` applied to an affine type, is AFFINE-CONTAINER-UNSUPPORTED.
  * == compares the declaration, every field and the children in order,
    recursively; both hashes read the same, in order. Two trees built
    differently but equal as trees are equal and hash equally.
  * Node[T] is Equatable or Hashable exactly when its other fields are:
    the child field adds no requirement (Node[int] is Hashable,
    Node[MutableVector[int]] is neither).
  * std::tree::walk(root, order) is a coroutine handing over every node
    (each subtree, root first, once per occurrence), the last as its
    result: Order::DepthFirst is preorder (a node, then its children's
    subtrees, first to last), Order::BreadthFirst every node at depth N
    before any at depth N + 1. Neither keeps a visited set.
  * No depth limit comes from the host: ==, the hashes, the printer and
    both walks keep their own work stacks (a chain of a million nodes
    compares, hashes and walks).

== WHY
A file tree, a document outline or a parse tree is a value: its meaning is
its contents and their order. Allowing exactly this recursive shape gives
recursive data without references, aliasing or cycles, and lets every
operation that compares, hashes or traverses walk it without recursion.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::coroutine
import std::mutable_vector
import std::tree

tree struct Node:
    label: str
    children: List[Node]

fn leaf(label: str) -> Node:
    Node {label: label, children: []}

nomethod fn labels(root: Node, order: std::tree::Order) -> List[str]:
    seen = std::mutable_vector::from_list([root.label])
    coroutine {step, first} = std::tree::walk(root, order)
    loop:
        if std::coroutine::done?(step):
            break
        seen.push(step().label)
    loop s in seen:
        s

t = Node {label: "root", children: [Node {label: "bb", children: [leaf("dd"), leaf("ee")]}, leaf("cc")]}
[labels(t, std::tree::Order::DepthFirst), labels(t, std::tree::Order::BreadthFirst)]
@caption Preorder and breadth-first traversal of one tree
@value [["root", "bb", "dd", "ee", "cc"], ["root", "bb", "cc", "dd", "ee"]]
@end

@example accept
tree struct Node:
    value: int
    children: List[Node]

a = Node {value: 1, children: [Node {value: 2, children: []}, Node {value: 3, children: []}]}
b = Node {value: 1, children: [Node {value: 3, children: []}, Node {value: 2, children: []}]}
[a == a, a == b, hash(a) == hash(a)]
@caption Children are ordered: reordering them makes another tree
@value [true, false, true]
@end

@example reject
tree struct Node:
    child: Node
    children: List[Node]

0
@caption A recursive field that is not the List of children
@error CORE SEMANTIC TREE-STRUCT-SHAPE
@at 2:12
@end

