@topic error.tree-struct-shape
title: TREE-STRUCT-SHAPE: exactly one recursive field, of type List[Self]
summary: A tree struct declaration must have exactly one field of type List[Self] and no other occurrence of its own type.
index: errors
alias: tree struct shape; recursive field; List[Self]
keywords: tree, recursive, children, List[Self], mutual recursion, Optional, map of Self
related: concept.tree-structs concept.structs error.tree-struct-combination
docs: TREE-STRUCT-MILESTONE-1.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC TREE-STRUCT-SHAPE

== MEANING
Reported at a tree struct's declaration when its recursion is not the one
shape a tree struct has: no field of type List[Self], a second one, the
declaration itself as a field (`child: Node`), nested deeper
(`List[List[Node]]`), inside another container (a MutableVector, a set, a
map, a function type), another application of a parameterized declaration
(`List[Node[int]]` in `Node[T]`), or reached again through another
declaration (mutual recursion).

== LIKELY CAUSES
  * a binary tree written with `left` and `right` fields;
  * an optional parent or child field;
  * two declarations meant to alternate (a directory of files of
    directories).

== WHAT TO DO
Keep the children in one `children: List[Node]` field: a binary node's two
children are that List's first and second elements, and alternating kinds
are one tree struct with an enum field saying which kind a node is. A
general recursive type that is no tree is an ordinary `struct`.

@example reject
tree struct Node:
    left: List[Node]
    right: List[Node]

0
@caption Two recursive fields
@error CORE SEMANTIC TREE-STRUCT-SHAPE
@at 3:12
@end

@example reject
tree struct A:
    bs: List[B]
    children: List[A]

tree struct B:
    as: List[A]
    children: List[B]

0
@caption Mutual recursion between two declarations
@error CORE SEMANTIC TREE-STRUCT-SHAPE
@at 2:9
@end

@topic error.tree-struct-combination
title: TREE-STRUCT-COMBINATION: no opaque, context or identity tree struct
summary: `tree` does not combine with `opaque`, `context` or an `identity` clause.
index: errors
alias: opaque tree struct; context tree struct; tree identity
keywords: tree, opaque, context, identity, combination
related: concept.tree-structs concept.opaque-structs concept.value-identity error.tree-struct-shape
docs: TREE-STRUCT-MILESTONE-1.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC TREE-STRUCT-COMBINATION

== MEANING
A tree's equality and hashing are its structure, so it has no identity
clause; a context is installed by its type and is no recursive value; and
std::tree::walk takes any tree apart into its subtrees, which for an opaque
struct only its owning module may do.

== WHAT TO DO
To hide a tree's representation, wrap it in an opaque struct of its own
(`opaque struct Outline: tree: Section`) and give the module the
operations its clients need.

@example reject
opaque tree struct Node:
    children: List[Node]

0
@caption An opaque tree struct
@error CORE SEMANTIC TREE-STRUCT-COMBINATION
@at 1:8
@end

